+### Login Lockout
+
+`POST /api/auth/login` counts consecutive failures on the user document. After
+5 (`AUTH_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS`) the account is locked for 15 minutes
+(`AUTH_LOCK_DURATION_MINUTES`) and further logins return `423 Locked` with a
+`Retry-After` header — checked *before* the bcrypt compare, so a locked account
+cannot be probed. Any successful login clears the counter and the lock.
+
+The rules live in `lib/loginLockout.js` as pure functions; the model persists
+them with an atomic `updateOne`. Already-issued JWTs are unaffected — the lock
+gates token *issuance* only, so existing sessions keep working.
+
+### Password Policy
+
+`lib/passwordPolicy.js` is the single source of truth for what counts as an
+acceptable password (currently: at least 6 characters). Pure functions again,
+so the rules are unit tested without Mongo. Three places read it:
+
+- `POST /api/auth/signup` — validates before creating the user
+- `POST /api/auth/password` — validates the new password
+- `models/User.js` — `minlength` on the schema, as a last line of defence
+
+### Change Password
+
+`POST /api/auth/password` lets a signed-in user replace their password:
+
+```
+requireAuth (Bearer JWT)
+  └─ load User by req.user.id
+       ├─ user.isLocked?              → 423 Locked  (same gate as /login)
+       ├─ comparePassword(current)?   → 401 + registerFailedLogin()
+       ├─ passwordPolicy.validate(new)→ 400
+       ├─ new === current             → 400
+       └─ user.password = new; user.save()   ← pre-save hook re-hashes
+            └─ resetLoginAttempts(); return a freshly signed token
+```
+
+Two deliberate choices:
+
+1. **The lockout applies here too.** This endpoint verifies a password, so
+   leaving it open would let a stolen token brute-force the current password
+   without ever touching `/login`. The check happens before the bcrypt compare,
+   and failures feed the same counter.
+2. **Existing sessions are not invalidated.** JWTs are stateless and there is no
+   revocation list, so tokens minted before the change keep working until they
+   expire. The response returns a new token purely so the calling client can
+   rotate. Session revocation on password change would need a token version or
+   denylist on the user document — not built.
+
